# About This Project
#
# Litex source:
# https://github.com/litexlang/golitex/tree/main/textbooks/The-Mechanics-of-Litex-Proof
#
# The Mechanics of Litex Proof is an ongoing Litex textbook motivated by
# Heather Macbeth's The Mechanics of Proof and its Lean-based approach to
# learning formal mathematics. This is an independent Litex case study, not an
# official adaptation, a replacement for the original book, or a claim that
# Litex reproduces Lean and Mathlib.
#
# Book conventions
#
# This is a Litex-first teaching book rather than a line-by-line translation of
# its motivating source. Examples and topics are selected and reorganized to
# teach Litex's fact-oriented proof style, and custom predicates sometimes
# restate familiar mathematics deliberately so readers can practise defining
# and reusing an interface. Builtin facts and the small imported standard
# library remain part of the checking environment; a successful example is
# therefore not a claim that every mathematical foundation was reconstructed
# inside its chapter.
#
# Pedagogical prose may suggest follow-up exercises without treating them as
# source-coverage obligations. Executable lessons themselves are expected to
# remain checked. If a future lesson requires an unfinished supporting result,
# it must expose that result as an explicit trust, axiom, abstract-proposition,
# or citation boundary rather than presenting it as verified. At present the
# book contains no executable trust, axiom, or abstract-proposition boundary.
#
# Coverage and verification should not be confused: the chosen lessons are a
# curriculum, not a claim to reproduce every topic or example in the motivating
# book or in Mathlib.
#
# The declarations currently included in this project are accepted by the
# Litex verifier. We often spell out reasoning that ordinary mathematical prose
# or the motivating source can leave implicit, because those intermediate facts
# help beginners see how a checked argument grows. If a supporting proof later
# proves too distracting or requires infrastructure beyond a chapter's scope,
# its boundary will remain deliberately visible under the convention above.
#
# The book progresses from calculation chains to reusable predicates, logical
# structure, induction, number theory, functions, sets, and relations. These
# examples make a direct comparison of proof interfaces possible: Lean commonly
# organizes interaction through terms, tactics, and a large library, whereas
# Litex asks the reader to write a sequence of mathematical facts and lets the
# verifier explain how the current context supports them. This is a comparison
# of design choices, not a claim that one system replaces the other.
#
# We hope readers learn practical proof patterns, become more precise about the
# hidden steps in ordinary mathematics, and experience formal proof as the
# gradual construction of a readable and reusable mathematical world.
